\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Loss weights of Eq.\nobreakspace {}\textup {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont (\ignorespaces \ref {eq:objective}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} in network training (full-resolution level; the Dice weight is 3, 4, and 5 at the three levels) and in instance optimisation (IO).}}{9}{table.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Effect of the PET and rigidity losses on the test set (28 patients, 38 pairs). Each network is trained with the given losses and evaluated as is (left) and with instance optimisation (IO) using the same losses (right). Mean$\pm $SD over patients. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: significantly better/worse than ``Neither'' in the same block (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$).}}{17}{table.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Contribution of each PET term in IO (test set, 28 patients): network trained without PET losses (T1), IO with all PET terms or with one term removed. Mean$\pm $SD over patients. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: significantly better/worse than with all terms (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$).}}{20}{table.3}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods.}}{21}{table.4}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Rigidity penalties compared (validation set, draft, 26 patients): network trained with the PET losses, IO with the PET terms and one rigidity penalty at the weight chosen on the validation set. Rigidity of bones is measured in three ways: the residual of a rigid fit to each bone (the quantity the rigid-fit penalty minimises), the Staring energy, i.e.\ the Staring penalty (properness, orthonormality, and affinity) on the whole bone (the quantity the Staring penalty minimises), and the distortion of short (5--15\nobreakspace {}mm) and long ($\geq $15\nobreakspace {}mm) within-bone distances (minimised by neither). Mean$\pm $SD over patients. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: significantly better/worse than without rigidity (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected over the three penalties per column, $p<0.05$).}}{25}{table.5}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces IO tuning on the validation set (network trained with the PET and rigidity losses). Stage 1: learning rate / iterations; stage 2: common scale of the four PET weights relative to the training-derived values. Candidates of a stage are ranked against each other with the significance-based ranking of the challenge (final score $100\cdot \mathrm {acc}^{0.4}\,\mathrm {PET}^{0.4}\,\mathrm {reg}^{0.2}$); chosen setting in bold. Metrics: patient means. The rigidity weight was fixed at 0.25 (Sec.\nobreakspace {}\ref {sect:tuning}).}}{34}{table.6}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Signed change of lesion volume and uptake caused by registration (test set, 26 patients with lesions): positive values mean that the warped lesions are larger or take up more. Methods as in Table\nobreakspace {}\ref {tab:baselines} (Training / IO: losses used; PET + rig.: PET-preservation and rigidity losses). Mean$\pm $SD and median over patients.}}{35}{table.7}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Volume and uptake change of ground-truth lesions, grouped by how much of each lesion the predicted lesion mask seen by IO covers (test set; network trained without PET losses, IO with the PET weights scaled by the given factor). Per scan, the change of the summed volume or uptake of the group's lesions relative to the unwarped moving scan, in \%; mean over the patients with lesions in the group.}}{35}{table.8}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Contribution of ground-truth lesions, grouped by how much of each lesion the predicted lesion mask seen by IO covers, to the volume and uptake change of the evaluation (test set; network trained without PET losses, IO with the PET weights scaled by the given factor). Contribution of a group: the change per scan if only the group's lesions changed and all other lesions kept their volume and uptake, in \%; mean over patients. All: the evaluation metric. Since changes of different lesions in a scan partly cancel, the contributions add up to slightly more.}}{36}{table.9}%
